<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 7: Core Principles  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/ch7-research.md):
  - Case study: how Kenya has written down its AI principles, 2025 to 2026.
    The main source is the July 2026 policy (226 pages, read from the PDF
    Lily supplied). The af.net "Responsible AI Framework" article was NOT
    used: we could not find it reported anywhere else.
  - Podcasts: 16 (regulation, 2023), 6 (Bletchley Park, 2023), 132 (Paris
    summit, 2025), 37 (Gemini images). Episode 35 is in Chapter 5.
  - Paris statement facts are from the Elysee page and Wikipedia.
  - The AI Bill 2026 details come from one secondary source.
  - Practise (STACK) section removed on purpose. Questions go in the exercises.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-core-principles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Core Principles</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-core-principles">
        <p>
            In August 2025, a news team asked the Social Health Authority how
            its means test works.
            SHA ignored the request.
            The team complained to the Ombudsman, who ordered SHA to answer.
            In November, SHA sent back the formula and a list of the
            questions it asks, but not the weights it gives the answers
            (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            Compare that with what Kenya's 2026 AI policy says.
            People are entitled to know when an AI system is acting on them
            (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            Almost everyone agrees with the idea of fair, transparent and
            accountable AI.
            Companies say it, governments say it and so do international
            summits.
            The hard questions come next.
            Who decides what <q>fair</q> means?
            Who makes sure it happens?
            And who benefits when it does?
        </p>

        <p>
            This chapter looks at the core principles of responsible AI, and at
            how different people and places define and enforce them, with
            Kenya as our main example.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Identify and define responsible AI principles like
                        fairness, accountability and transparency.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Compare how different organisations and countries apply
                        them.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain how context, such as culture, politics and
                        economics, changes which principles are put first.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate a real case using these principles.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. Case study                                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-core-principles-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: Kenya Writes Down Its Principles</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-timeline">
            <title>Four documents in eighteen months</title>

            <p>
                Kenya has been working on its approach to AI governance for a
                while.
                Here are the main steps.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tbl-core-principles-timeline">
                <title>Kenya's AI governance, 2025 to 2026</title>
                <tabular>
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>When</cell>
                        <cell>What</cell>
                        <cell>What kind of document</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>January 2025</cell>
                        <cell>A workshop in Nairobi on the draft Kenya AI
                        Statement of Principles, hosted by the Ministry of ICT
                        with KICTANet and with UK support</cell>
                        <cell>A statement of five principles, still being
                        developed</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>March 2025</cell>
                        <cell>The Kenya AI Strategy 2025 to 2030</cell>
                        <cell>A national strategy</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>April 2026</cell>
                        <cell>The Artificial Intelligence Bill, 2026, introduced in the Senate</cell>
                        <cell>A proposed law, not in force</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>July 2026</cell>
                        <cell>The Kenya AI and Other Emerging Technologies Policy</cell>
                        <cell>A national policy, which says it will be backed by a
                        new Act</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                The January 2025 workshop brought together at least 100 people
                from government, industry, universities and civil society.
                The five principles in the Statement of Principles were Safe
                AI, Sustainable AI, Secure AI, Trustworthy AI and Inclusive AI
                (<xref ref="biblio-kictanet-2025" text="custom">Mujesia, 2025</xref>).
                The strategy was due to be launched on 27 March 2025
                (<xref ref="biblio-ict-2025" text="custom">Ministry of ICT, 2025</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The Bill was sponsored by nominated Senator Karen Nyamu.
                It proposes an Office of the AI Commissioner.
                In October 2026, a Senate committee recommended that the Senate
                shouldn't proceed with it, and that the government should first
                finalise a national AI policy
                (<xref ref="biblio-star-2026" text="custom">The Star, 2026</xref>).
                The other details of the Bill, like its dates, come from a single
                secondary source
                (<xref ref="biblio-fukazee-2026" text="custom">Maina, 2026</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-sixteen">
            <title>Sixteen guiding principles</title>

            <p>
                The July 2026 policy is the longest of the four, at 226 pages.
                It sets out sixteen guiding principles
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
                Here they are, in plain words.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tbl-core-principles-sixteen">
                <title>The guiding principles in Kenya's 2026 AI policy</title>
                <tabular>
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Principle</cell>
                        <cell>In plain words</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>1. Human rights-based approach</cell>
                        <cell>Protect constitutional rights, and reduce discrimination,
                        exclusion and unlawful surveillance.
                        It lists fairness, transparency, accountability,
                        contestability and access to redress, with extra
                        protection for children and vulnerable groups.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>2. Human-centredness and meaningful human control</cell>
                        <cell>People keep real control over systems that affect
                        their rights, safety or access to essential services,
                        with human review in higher-risk cases.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>3. Protection of human agency</cell>
                        <cell>Systems shouldn't manipulate or deceive people, or
                        exploit their weaknesses to keep them hooked.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>4. Risk-based and proportionate regulation</cell>
                        <cell>The higher the risk, the stronger the rules.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>5. Safety, security and robustness</cell>
                        <cell>Safe by design, tested and monitored, with a plan
                        for when things go wrong.
                        Safety includes bias, misinformation and labour
                        exploitation.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>6. Transparency, accountability and the right to know</cell>
                        <cell>Systems that affect people must be explainable and
                        auditable, and people are entitled to know when an AI is
                        acting on them.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>7. Inclusion and equity</cell>
                        <cell>Reduce gaps in access across place, gender,
                        disability, age, language and income.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>8. Sustainability</cell>
                        <cell>Use energy and water efficiently, and deal with
                        electronic waste responsibly.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>9. Innovation enablement and a competitive ecosystem</cell>
                        <cell>Make the rules clear enough to encourage
                        responsible experiments and investment.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>10. Public value and state capability</cell>
                        <cell>Government use of AI should improve services, with
                        accountability, oversight and redress.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>11. Public participation and democratic governance</cell>
                        <cell>Affected communities, workers and others get a say,
                        and there should be ways to complain.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>12. Sovereignty and strategic autonomy</cell>
                        <cell>Kenya keeps oversight of its own data and
                        infrastructure, while staying open to partnerships.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>13. Local value creation</cell>
                        <cell>Build Kenyan skills, data, language resources and
                        businesses, and avoid dependency.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>14. Community and mutual responsibility (Harambee)</cell>
                        <cell>AI should strengthen community well-being, social
                        trust and shared prosperity, with particular attention to
                        rural and traditionally organized communities.</cell>
                        </row>
                        
                        <row>
                        <cell>15. Interoperability and open standards</cell>
                        <cell>Where feasible, use designs and standards that let
                        systems work together, so that people and governments
                        aren't locked in to one vendor.</cell>
                        </row>
                        
                        <row>
                        <cell>16. Do no harm</cell>
                        <cell>Prevent harm to people, communities, institutions and
                        the environment.
                        Where risks can't be reduced enough, use of a system shall
                        be restricted, suspended or prohibited.</cell>
                        </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                The words fairness, accountability and transparency are in
                there, mostly under principles 1 and 6.
                But the list is much wider than those three.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-context">
            <title>What's particular to Kenya?</title>

            <p>
                The policy says that it's informed by the OECD AI Principles
                of 2019, UNESCO's Recommendation on the Ethics of AI of 2021,
                the African Union's Continental AI Strategy and the UN's Global
                Digital Compact
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
                So a lot of it will look familiar.
            </p>

            <p>
                A few things stand out as Kenyan, or as shaped by Kenya's
                position.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Sovereignty and local value.</em>
                        Principles 12 and 13 are about who controls data and
                        infrastructure, and who benefits from what's built on
                        them.
                        That's a very different concern from a country that
                        hosts the big AI companies.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Harambee.</em>
                        Principle 14 draws on a Kenyan idea of pulling together,
                        and puts the community, and not just the individual, at
                        the centre.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Language.</em>
                        The policy asks for local languages in systems that
                        affect public services, and says datasets should respect
                        Kenya's linguistic diversity.
                        It also says that people must be told they're dealing
                        with an AI in a language they understand.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>The people behind the data.</em>
                        It names labour exploitation as a safety risk, and
                        proposes to recognise Kenya's data annotation and
                        labelling sector as a national asset.
                        Think back to the workers in Chapters 2 and 3.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-who">
            <title>Who defines it, and who would enforce it?</title>

            <p>
                The policy was written by the State Department for ICT and the
                Digital Economy.
                The annex on how it was developed describes roundtables,
                multi-stakeholder dialogues, public submissions and interviews
                with experts.
                It says the draft was reviewed for legal soundness by ILINA
                and by the Office of the Attorney General
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Enforcement is mostly still to come.
                The policy proposes the following.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A National AI and Other Emerging Technologies Council,
                        to coordinate and oversee.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A new AI Governance Act.
                        It would set up a hybrid system, with central
                        coordination and standards, and sector regulators doing
                        the day-to-day enforcement.
                        The policy gives an indicative timeline of drafting the
                        Act within six months, and enacting it within twelve.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A Kenya AI Safety Institute for testing, evaluation and
                        red-teaming.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A stronger Office of the Data Protection Commissioner,
                        which already enforces the Data Protection Act.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                The policy also says that it doesn't override the Constitution
                or existing laws such as the Data Protection Act
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
                Until the new Act exists, it sets a direction, but it isn't a
                law.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-test">
            <title>Putting the principles to the test</title>

            <p>
                Here's a thought experiment.
                SHA's means test started in October 2024, before this policy
                existed, so nothing was broken.
                But what would the policy's principles ask of SHA, if they
                applied?
                (The policy leaves out simple systems that don't use
                statistical learning.
                The means test is a regression built on survey data, which
                Lighthouse calls a simple form of machine learning, so it
                probably counts.)
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tbl-core-principles-sha">
                <title>What Kenya's 2026 policy asks, and what happened with SHA</title>
                <tabular>
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>What the policy says</cell>
                        <cell>What we know about SHA</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>People are entitled to know when an AI system is
                        acting on them (principle 6).</cell>
                        <cell>SHA ignored a request for how the test works,
                        until the Ombudsman ordered it to respond, and it didn't
                        give the weights
                        (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Public bodies must provide human review, appeal and
                        redress where a decision significantly affects access to
                        services (section 3.5.6).</cell>
                        <cell>Florence's appeal through an automated line was
                        turned down without a reason
                        (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                        The High Court ordered a clear and accessible complaints
                        mechanism in March 2026
                        (<xref ref="biblio-capital-2026" text="custom">Wakaya, 2026</xref>).</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Public bodies must carry out an impact assessment
                        before deploying a high-risk system, including one that
                        affects access to health services (section 3.5.6).</cell>
                        <cell>IDinsight reviewed the model just before launch and
                        found it unfair to low-income households
                        (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
                        The launch went ahead.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Datasets should be documented, and should be
                        representative and of good quality (section 3.6.1).</cell>
                        <cell>The survey behind the model was out of date, and
                        had few of the poorest households in it
                        (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Look at what the principles do here.
                They name the problems we found in Chapters 4 and 6, and they
                ask for the right things.
                What they can't do is make anything happen.
                That depends on an Act, a regulator and people who know their
                rights.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-lessons">
            <title>What the case shows</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Principles are easy to agree on and hard to enforce.
                        Kenya has written down a lot, but most of the machinery
                        is still to come.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Context changes the list.
                        Sovereignty, local value and Harambee are there
                        because of where Kenya sits in the world of AI.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Who writes the principles matters.
                        This policy went through consultation, but the people
                        most affected by a means test may not have been in the
                        room.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A principle you can't use is a promise.
                        <q>The right to know</q> only helps if you can find out
                        that a system is being used on you.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Podcast 16: regulation                                      -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-core-principles-regulation">
        <title>Does Regulation Help?</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-core-principles-regulation">
            <p>
                This section comes from a conversation between me and David
                Stern, in 2023
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-016" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 16, 2023</xref>).
                It's about half an hour long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/016-responsible-ai-regulation/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-core-principles-podcast-016">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 16: Responsible AI, Regulation</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-core-principles-podcast-016"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3062/016-responsible-ai-regulation.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-amazon">
            <title>The recruiting tool</title>

            <p>
                I start with an example, which David names as Amazon's.
                As I tell it, a company built a tool to sift through CVs, and it
                marked women down, because it had learned from past hiring.
                Removing gender from the data didn't fix it.
                Other words, like a mention of a women's chess team,
                still carried the signal.
                Amazon dropped the project after a large investment.
                (That's the proxy problem from Chapter 6.)
            </p>

            <p>
                David's view is that Amazon had too much reputation to lose,
                and dropped it because the risk of being seen as sexist
                outweighed the benefit.
                I wondered whether a smaller company would have cut its losses
                in the same way.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-reputation">
            <title>Why would big companies want rules?</title>

            <p>
                David's argument is that regulation is neither good nor bad for
                business on its own.
                Good regulation helps good businesses.
                Big companies have a reputation to protect, so they'd mostly
                build unbiased, transparent systems anyway.
                The difference is with start-ups, which can get away with
                fixing the problems later.
                A regulator who asked for tests and a level of transparency
                before release could catch problems like the gender bias
                early.
            </p>

            <p>
                He thinks the EU is furthest ahead, and notes that the EU,
                Chinese and US approaches look fairly similar to him, which he
                reads as a growing consensus.
                Then he points out the irony.
                Rules designed to restrain big tech could end up favouring it,
                because it can afford to comply.
            </p>

            <p>
                At the start of the episode I mention that the head of OpenAI
                had said the company would stop operating in places where it
                couldn't comply with the rules.
                David reads that as aimed at the EU.
                (I haven't checked it.)
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-regulation-unknown">
            <title>What he doesn't know</title>

            <p>
                David is honest that he's an optimist, but he has no idea what
                will happen.
                He says there's no way of running an experiment to see what
                would have happened under other rules.
                He gives two scenarios that worry him: regulation could shut
                whole regions out of AI tools, or it could drive illegal AI
                that overtakes the regulated kind.
                He also thinks that responsible AI will win out over twenty
                years, with or without regulation.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-regulation-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Regulation changes incentives.
                        What it requires, and who owns the process, decides
                        whether it helps.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Reputation protects people from big companies, more than
                        from small ones.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Removing a sensitive variable doesn't remove a bias.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 3. Podcast 6: Bletchley                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-core-principles-bletchley">
        <title>What Are We Afraid Of?</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-core-principles-bletchley">
            <p>
                The first international AI Safety Summit was held at Bletchley
                Park in the UK, in November 2023.
                David and I talked about it afterwards
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-006" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 6, 2023</xref>).
                It's about 25 minutes long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/006-bletchley-park-ai-safety-summit/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-core-principles-podcast-006">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 6: Bletchley Park AI Safety Summit</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-core-principles-podcast-006"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/2937/006-bletchley-park-ai-safety-summit.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-killer-robots">
            <title>Far-off risks and present ones</title>

            <p>
                David's main criticism is that the summit spent too much time on
                <q>killer robots</q>, and not enough on harms that are happening
                now.
                He says bias is the real current risk.
                The real safety issue now, in his view, is governments using AI,
                and he points to the Dutch childcare system and to a recent
                announcement about the UK using AI in policing.
            </p>

            <p>
                He says the summit framed AI as an existential threat, and that
                distracted from those present harms.
                He's less worried about AI taking over, partly because
                consciousness isn't well defined.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-safe-responsible">
            <title>Safe and responsible</title>

            <p>
                I pointed out that the summit was about safety, which I'd take
                to include responsibility.
                David replied that he'd hope that if you're responsible, you're
                safe, but that safety goes further.
            </p>

            <p>
                When I asked whether the risks sit in the data, the learning
                or the algorithm, David said it's not just those three things,
                and that the risks are everywhere.
                Interpretation, management and human factors matter too.
                Anyone who says they can do this safely, he says, isn't
                thinking hard enough.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-outcome">
            <title>What came out of it?</title>

            <p>
                The one outcome David mentions is an agreement that there would be
                another summit.
                He found it disappointing, but not surprising, and compares it
                to the COP climate summits.
                He thinks the regulations being proposed were really rather
                sensible, and that the worry about stifling innovation is real,
                but not big.
                He also says big tech in the US accepting that AI needs
                regulation was unheard of.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 4. Podcast 132: Paris                                          -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-core-principles-paris">
        <title>A Statement of Intent</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-core-principles-paris">
            <p>
                In February 2025, the next summit took place in Paris.
                We talked about it the day after
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-132" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 132, 2025</xref>).
                It's about 20 minutes long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/132-paris-ai-action-summit/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-core-principles-podcast-132">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 132: Paris AI Action Summit</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-core-principles-podcast-132"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/6473/132-paris-ai-action-summit.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-paris-statement">
            <title>What was signed</title>

            <p>
                The summit produced a text called the Statement on Inclusive and
                Sustainable Artificial Intelligence for People and the Planet.
                The French government's page lists 63 countries, plus the African
                Union Commission and the European Union, among the signatories.
                Kenya is one of them.
                The US and the UK didn't sign
                (<xref ref="biblio-elysee-2025" text="custom">Government of France, 2025</xref>).
                The UK said that it didn't go far enough on global governance and
                national security
                (<xref ref="biblio-wikipedia-paris" text="custom">Wikipedia, 2025</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Its priorities include making AI accessible to reduce digital
                divides, making it open, inclusive, transparent, ethical, safe,
                secure and trustworthy, avoiding the concentration of the
                market, and making it sustainable.
                The text doesn't say it's binding, and it doesn't describe any way
                of enforcing it
                (<xref ref="biblio-elysee-2025" text="custom">Government of France, 2025</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-paris-david">
            <title>What David made of it</title>

            <p>
                David says that the statement matters as a statement of intent,
                but that documents like this <q>don't actually mean anything</q>
                unless laws put them into practice.
            </p>

            <p>
                He calls it a big, important step forward on responsible AI.
                He notes that China signed, and that the EU's influence is
                strong.
                And he says that the same words, open, inclusive, transparent,
                ethical, safe and sustainable, could be built into funding
                and into what governments buy.
                That's his expectation, not something that has happened.
                He compares it with earlier climate documents that had no
                consequences, but thinks this one is different.
            </p>

            <p>
                For Kenya, this is the same pattern as the 2026 policy.
                The words are agreed.
                What happens next depends on who turns them into rules, money
                and checks.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Podcast 37: Gemini                                          -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-core-principles-gemini">
        <title>Biased or Absurd?</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-core-principles-gemini">
            <p>
                In Chapter 5 we looked at AI image tools that left people out.
                What happens when a company tries to fix that, and it goes
                wrong?
                This is the subject of another conversation between me and
                David
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-037" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 37, 2024</xref>).
                It's about half an hour long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/037-generating-ai-images-between-bias-and-absurdity/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-core-principles-podcast-037">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 37: Generating AI Images, Between Bias and Absurdity</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-core-principles-podcast-037"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3527/037-generating-ai-images-between-bias-and-absurdity.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-gemini-google">
            <title>What Google said</title>

            <p>
                In February 2024, Google switched off its Gemini tool's ability
                to make images of people.
                Users had posted images that were historically wrong.
                Google's senior vice president, Prabhakar Raghavan, explained
                two failures.
                First, the tuning that was meant to show a range of people didn't
                allow for prompts that should clearly <em>not</em> show a range.
                Second, the model became much more cautious than intended, and
                refused some prompts it should have answered.
                He said that Google hadn't wanted either result
                (<xref ref="biblio-google-2024" text="custom">Raghavan, 2024</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-core-principles-gemini-david">
            <title>What we said</title>

            <p>
                In the episode I give two examples that went round at the time.
                One was a Black founding father of the US, and the other was
                people of many races in pictures of German soldiers in the Second
                World War.
                David says that the tool overcompensated by adding diversity
                where it shouldn't.
                He calls AI <q>a very blunt tool</q>.
            </p>

            <p>
                He says that in his opinion, Google's pause was a political
                decision, and that it was seen as an attempt to do no harm,
                partly because Google worried about its reputation.
                And he says that changing history is irresponsible.
            </p>

            <p>
                So what should a tool aim for: accuracy, or fairness?
                The questions at the end of this chapter ask you to decide.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 6. Illustrative example                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-core-principles-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Read the Policy</title>

        <p>
            Kenya's 2026 policy is on the ICT ministry's website.
            It's long, but the parts we've used are short.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find section 1.6, the guiding principles.
                    Read principle 6.
                    What are you entitled to know?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Think of a digital service you've used in Kenya, such as a
                    bank or phone company chatbot, a loan app, or a government
                    portal.
                    Did it tell you that you were dealing with an AI, or that a
                    system had made a decision about you?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find section 3.5.6.
                    Pick one of its requirements for public bodies.
                    How would you check whether a public service that you know
                    meets it?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Write down one question that you'd ask the service, and who
                    you'd ask.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Please don't give any private details to a chatbot or service while
            you try this.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 7. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-core-principles">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-1">
            <title>In your own words</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a sentence each to define fairness, accountability and
                    transparency.
                    Then give an example from this course of an AI system that
                    fell short on each one.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-2">
            <title>Expected and surprising</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Look at the sixteen principles in Kenya's 2026 policy.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Which would you expect to find in any country's
                            policy?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Which are particular to Kenya's situation?
                            Why?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Is there one you'd add, or one that you'd put first?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-3">
            <title>Who decides?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who should decide what counts as <q>fair</q> for an AI
                            system used on Kenyans?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who should check that it's followed?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who benefits from the answer, and who might lose?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Which voices do you think were missing from the
                            consultation, and how would you include them?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-4">
            <title>Kenya and Paris</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compare Kenya's 2026 policy with the Paris statement, which
                    Kenya signed.
                    What do they have in common?
                    What's in one but not the other?
                    How might the position of a country like Kenya, compared with
                    a country that hosts the big AI companies, explain the
                    differences?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-5">
            <title>Words on paper</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David says that documents like the Paris statement don't mean
                    anything unless laws put them into practice.
                    Pick one of Kenya's principles.
                    Describe what would have to exist, such as a law, a
                    regulator, a penalty or a way to complain, for it to mean
                    something.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-6">
            <title>The right to know</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The policy says that people should be told, in a language they
                    understand, when an automated system makes or shapes an
                    important decision about them.
                    It also says that being told doesn't replace the right to
                    human review, explanation and redress.
                    Write the message that you think SHA should show a household
                    when it assesses its premium.
                    What should it say, and what should it offer?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-7">
            <title>Test a case</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose a case from the course, such as the Tala loan app, the
                    SHA means test, the AI detectors or the Amazon hiring tool.
                    Take principles 1 and 6 from the policy.
                    What would each of them ask of that system?
                    Which would it already meet, and which not?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-8">
            <title>Reputation as a safeguard</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David's argument is that Amazon dropped its recruiting tool
                    because it had too much reputation to lose.
                    I wondered whether a smaller company would have done the same.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Does reputation protect people from a small
                            lender or app?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Why might rules written to restrain big companies
                            end up helping them?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-9">
            <title>Accuracy or fairness?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Gemini was changed to show a range of people, and then it made
                    historically wrong pictures.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Should AI tools aim for accuracy, or for fairness?
                            Can both be achieved?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who should decide?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Is it worse for a tool to be biased, or to be
                            absurd?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-core-principles-10">
            <title>Correcting an injustice</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Kenya's 2026 policy has a do no harm principle (number 16).
                    It says that where risks can't be reduced enough, a system's use
                    shall be restricted, suspended or prohibited.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                    <li>
                    When does an attempt to correct an injustice end up
                    creating misinformation?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Where's the balance between doing no harm, and trying to
                            do good, with an AI tool?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Think back to the image tools in Chapter 5, which left
                            some people out.
                            Is the fix that went wrong with Gemini a reason not
                            to try?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

